    thinking: "**Designing the Tabs**\n\nI\'m now focusing on designing the three requested tabs aesthetically. My current thought is to start with the first tab, **\344\270\273\351\241\265** (Home). I plan to make it a central hub displaying today\'s chores and tasks, specifically the \"\345\275\223\345\211\215\350\246\201\345\201\232\347\232\204\344\272\213\346\203\205\" (Must-Do core task checklist, e.g., today\'s goals, week\'s goals).\n\n\n**Refining the First Tab**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Home\" tab\'s features. I\'ve decided on a very clean, minimalist look. The core will be a task display (\"\345\275\223\345\211\215\350\246\201\345\201\232\347\232\204\344\272\213\346\203\205\") with filter toggles and a quick-add option. Crucially, I\'m designing a prominent \"Check-In\" button to trigger a fully animated modal. This modal will feature four sub-forms, including \"Sleep\" (time, rating, notes) and \"Diet,\" complete with an auto-incrementing meal counter and photo upload/annotation (potentially using AI).\n\n\n**Integrating Daily Logs**\n\nI\'m now integrating the daily check-in logs directly into the \"Home\" tab. Displaying the user\'s completed activities (sleep, meals, workouts, study) as a visual list immediately after logging feels very satisfying and shows them their instant accomplishments. This means I\'ll need to design how this new feed will display. I am also working on the structure of the next two tabs: \"Login and Register\" and \"Settings\".\n\n\n**Defining the Authentication Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user authentication in the second tab. I\'m incorporating username/password login and registration. If logged in, the tab will display login status, avatar/ID, and account switching/profile viewing options. To maintain state, I plan to store user profiles and associated task sets in simulated LocalStorage.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create a highly polished modular `/src/components/CheckInModal.tsx` component to handle the \"\346\211\223\345\215\241\" check-in pop-up, containing the sleep, diet, workout, and learning check-in widgets. It defaults to showing \"\344\273\212\346\227\245\347\254\254 N \351\241\277\" for meals, allows camera file imports analyzed by our visual neural API, and offers full validation and micro-animations."
